    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Historians disagree whether the khanate began in 1437 or 1438, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulugh-muhammad">Ulugh Muhammad</a> took power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a>, or in 1445, when his son Maxmud began to rule there.</li><li>From 1487 to 1521 the khanate was in effect a Muscovite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>, and its nobility was split between pro-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> and pro-Crimean groups.</li><li>Ivan the Terrible's forces stormed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a> on 2 October 1552 after a siege of more than 40 days, and most of the defenders were killed and the population massacred.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>No document of the khanate survived the Russian conquest, and the records of the early Russian administration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a> were destroyed during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/time-of-troubles">Time of Troubles</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

