    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The khanate grew out of the former lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a> Ulus or Kazan Duchy, which may have regained a degree of independence within the disintegrating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> by the turn of the 15th century. Its territory comprised the Bulgar-populated duchies of Bolğar, Cükätäw, Kazan and Qaşan, and covered present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatarstan">Tatarstan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a> El, Chuvashia, Mordovia and parts of Udmurtia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bashkortostan">Bashkortostan</a>.</p><p>Historians disagree about the founding. One view dates it to 1437 or 1438, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulugh-muhammad">Ulugh Muhammad</a>, a former khan of the Golden Horde, took power in Kazan with the support of the local nobility. According to the Great Russian Encyclopedia, most sources support a second view, which dates the khanate from autumn 1445, when his son Maxmud began to rule in Kazan. The History of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> concludes that none of the versions is confirmed by even two independent sources. Under Ulugh Muhammad and Maxmud, Kazan forces raided Muscovy several times.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Most of the khanate was forest, with only the south adjoining the steppe. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>, Kama and Vyatka were its main rivers and trade routes. The urban population worked in pottery, woodworking, leatherworking, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-technology">ironworking</a>, arms-making, weaving and jewelry, in towns such as Qazan, Alabuğa, Arça, Bolğar and Cükätaw. The khanate traded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, exporting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leather">leather goods</a>, honey and grain and importing luxury goods, weapons, textiles and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a>. It minted no coins and mainly used Russian ones. The Taşayaq fair in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a> was the main fair of the Volga region.</p><p>Slaves were also exported. The khan and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> lords held many prisoners of war as slaves; according to the Chuvash encyclopedia, 100,000 Russian captives were held in the khanate between the 1520s and the 1540s, and when Russian slaves were freed in 1551 about 60,000 people returned home by official figures. Captives, especially women, were sold to harems around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a>. The population included Tatars, Chuvash, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mordvins">Mordvins</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udmurts">Udmurts</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bashkirs">Bashkirs</a>, and the tax-paying class, the qara xalıq, comprised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-peasant">state peasants</a>, dependent people and prisoners of war.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The army was made up of levies from the districts and towns, the personal troops of the khan and the nobility, and allied troops. Estimates of its size differ: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> encyclopedia gives 30,000 to 50,000 men, the Chuvash encyclopedia up to 60,000. Firearms were used in field battles and in the defence of fortifications, and Russian sources record the defenders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a> firing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a>, handguns and bows from the walls. Tatar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> troops defended the khanate from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nogais">Nogais</a>, who more often raided the farming Tatars and Chuvash than recognised the khan.</p><p>Kazan's wars were chiefly with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. In 1445 Maxmud and Yakub captured Vasily II near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzdal">Suzdal</a>, who agreed to pay a ransom and an annual tribute. In 1521 Sahib Giray led a successful campaign against Moscow, at the same time as his brother, the Crimean khan Mehmed I Giray, attacked it. From 1545 Moscow organised a series of campaigns against Kazan, and in 1552 the forces of Ivan the Terrible, operating from the fortress of Sviyazhsk, built in 1551, laid the final siege.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The khan was formally the sole ruler, but real power lay with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwan">diwan</a>, an assembly of the highest religious and secular aristocracy. Major matters of state, including the enthronement and deposition of khans and declarations of war and peace, were decided at assemblies of the nobility (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurultai">kurultai</a>). The highest nobility came from four ruling clans, Shirin, Barin, Arghin and Qipchaq, joined by the Manghit clan in the 1540s and 1550s. The military service nobility included the uğlans, bäks, ämirs, morzas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni Islam</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanafi-school">Hanafi school</a> was the state religion. The head of the clergy was the säyet (sayyid), and sheikhs, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qadi">qadis</a>, mullahs and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imams</a> led worship and judged civil cases. The khanate was divided into large districts, the daruğas; the Great Russian Encyclopedia lists five, while other works give four or six or seven. The throne changed hands often: Mikhail Khudyakov counted 15 khans in 118 years, some reigning two or three times, and Şahğäli reigned three times.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Khudyakov described the khanate as formed from an older <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgar</a> base, with its population and culture, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> element that brought the state organisation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> Turki (Old Tatar) was the official language of administration and diplomatic correspondence, and Prince Andrey Kurbsky wrote that Mordvin, Chuvash, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a>, Udmurt and Bashkir were spoken there as well.</p><p>Literacy among the Tatars was fairly widespread, because every village with a mosque also had a school. A large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a> and library stood at the cathedral mosque in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a>, and Kazan became a centre of science and theology. Old Tatar poets and writers included Möxämmädyar, whom the History of the Tatars calls the great poet of the khanate, and Qolşärif. The ruined city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolghar">Bolghar</a> became a religious and educational centre and a place of pilgrimage. White-stone architecture and stone carving reached a high level. The khanate's trade made Kazan's fair the main fair of the Volga region until, in 1525, Safa Giray agreed at Russian insistence to move it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizhny-novgorod">Nizhny Novgorod</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Conflict between pro-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> and pro-Crimean nobles marked the last decade. Safa Giray, deposed in 1546, returned with Nogai troops and overthrew Şahğäli; after his death in 1549 his two-year-old son Ütämeşgäräy reigned under the regent Söyembikä. In 1551 the nobility accepted Russian terms, but an uprising led by the sayyid Qolşärif expelled the Russian garrison in 1552, and Yadegar Mokhammad became khan.</p><p>In 1552 the forces of Ivan the Terrible besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a>. After more than 40 days the Russians blew up the walls and stormed the city on 2 October 1552 (Old Style); most defenders were killed and the population was massacred. The administration of the khanate was wiped out, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> were resettled away from rivers, roads and Kazan, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> clergy such as Germogen forcibly baptised many Tatars. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a> resisted until May 1557. The Prikaz of the Kazan Palace undertook the forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russification">Russification</a> and Christianization of the Tatars and other peoples, and the term Tsardom of Kazan was used until 1708.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khanate-of-kazan">Khanate of Kazan</a> was a multiethnic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> state on the Middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>, heir to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> and to Volga <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. Its khans were weak against a powerful nobility and torn between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>, and its towns lived by crafts, trade and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> until Ivan the Terrible conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a> in 1552.</em></p>

